My objection isn't that it's unbalanced, just that I'm having a hard
time seeing why the game works that way (either a gameplay reason or
a role-playing reason).  It's such an obscure situation that IMHO it
doesn't make much of a difference as far as balancing theft goes.

I could maybe see the logic of it in cases such as when you drop your
stuff due to lycanthropy, as the shopkeeper wouldn't be likely to
converse with a wolf/rat/jackal/whatever as to whether the animal
wanted to sell (but then again, he wouldn't likely bother paying the
animal either, and if that were the rationale, a player poly'd into
an insufficiently-intelligent monster shouldn't be able to do
business with the shopkeeper, and an intelligent pet shouldn't be
able to steal).

Automatically selling when you throw/shoot things also makes some
sense, as the shopkeeper may be somewhat upset about fighting going
on in the shop, and thus less willing to be polite and ask whether
you meant to sell.  Simply dropping an item accidentally shouldn't
bother the shopkeeper, though.

